DONE SINCE 0.9.1
================

- Unit tests. 'make test' runs tests/run.sh (one case per documented
  behaviour and per bug that was once real), tests/api.c (the library entry
  points, linked directly), and tests/oracle.py (brute-forces the alphabet
  and compares whole sets against Python's re). 'make test-asan' repeats all
  of it under AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and
  LeakSanitizer, with leak detection on.
- Flag to change the enumeration direction: (?L), (?-L) and -L.
- make install now installs librxe.a and rxe.h, so the library can actually
  be linked against.
- Fifteen correctness fixes, from an uninitialised status byte that made
  every parse report a garbage error, through a double free on any
  backreference, to backreference numbering being off by one after every
  (?:...) group. See the git log.


SHORT TERM TO-DOs
=================

- Write API documentation. rxe.h has the data structures commented but
  there is no account of the calling sequence: parse, seek, current,
  iterate, free. tests/api.c is currently the closest thing to one.
- Implement missing things: POSIX character classes, \o, \g with negative
  and named backreferences, \k, etc.
- Decide the caseless backreference question. '(?i)(a)\1' generates
  {aa, AA}, while Perl's matcher accepts {aa, aA, Aa, AA}, since under /i a
  backreference compares case-insensitively. Exact copies are probably the
  more useful reading for generation, but the divergence should either be
  fixed or written down in the manual page.
- Code refactoring
  > split handle_repeats in two functions: the first parses/validates
    the paramaters, the second does the actual work
  > simplify the ? quantifier using the above function as a special case {0,1}
    (note: since the ? quantifier and handle_repeats have twice now had the
    same bug fixed separately -- clearing the node's own characters, and
    inheriting the enumeration direction -- this is worth more than it looks)
- Error handling
  > handle_repeats still signals failure by writing a status code into the
    mpz_t it was given for the result. Clean that up.
  > More descriptive error messages pointing to the probable location


LONG-TERM TO-DOs
================

> Replace the node-cloning repetition with closed-form counted nodes.
  handle_repeats currently materialises {r0,r1} by cloning the node once per
  repetition per count, which costs O(m^2) memory: a{1,2000} takes 316MB and
  a{1,20000} would want about 30GB. A repetition node carrying a count
  instead makes the cardinality a geometric series and the seek a division.

> Enumerate infinite sets. Keep reporting that the set is infinite, but
  still map integers onto its members, so * and + stop being a hard error.
  Each unbounded quantifier is an N-valued dimension; the finite part stays
  the mixed-radix numeral it is today, and a pairing function spreads one
  index across the dimensions. Best done together with the closed-form
  repetition above rather than after it: an unbounded quantifier is just a
  counted repetition whose upper bound is infinite, so the two are one
  change to the same arithmetic.

> Permute the enumeration order under a key. A keyed bijection on [0,N)
  applied to the index before seeking gives a different, deterministic,
  repeatable order -- enough to randomise a brute-force search without
  losing the ability to resume it. A small Feistel network over the index
  with cycle walking is the standard construction. This touches nothing but
  the index, so it can be done at any point, independently of everything
  above, and it subsumes -r: a permutation visits every member exactly once
  instead of sampling with replacement.

> JIT compiler

> Binary tree over alternations, to replace the linear scan in rxe_seek.
  Deferred, and possibly not worth doing: 20,000 hand-written alternatives
  already parse and seek in 0.02s, and enumerate in full in 0.02s. The only
  place large alternation counts arise in practice is handle_repeats, which
  builds one alternation per repeat count -- and the closed-form rework
  above removes them. Revisit only if this ever shows up in a profile.
